WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:53.000
This is 99% Invisible. I'm Roman Mars. In 2010, a history of the world in 100 Objects hosted by Neil McGregor premiered on BBC Radio 4 to great acclaim. It was a groundbreaking series that told a complex and interconnected history of the world through 100 objects housed in the British Museum collection. For 2026, BBC Studios decided they wanted to revamp and reimagine that ambitious series, this time focusing on the history of the history of the U.S. United States, coinciding with the 250th anniversary of the signing of the Declaration of Independence. To the great credit of the BBC, they thought that maybe British broadcasters weren't the best equipped to take on the curation of 100 objects that told the history of the United States. So that's when they reached out to me, lifelong American, Roman Mars.

2
00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:41.000
With our 100 Object series, we knew we wanted to get out of the museum and focus on objects that were overlooked, thrown away, mass produced and misunderstood, not priceless museum pieces, but the everyday things that provide a portal into stories about what America means to itself and to the world at large. But from the beginning, I knew I needed sage advice to do the job right. And so I sought the council of the original 100 Objects host, Neil McGregor. Here's our conversation. What I was hoping you could do to begin with is taking back to January 2010, a history of the world in 100 objects is launching. It starts with a mummy that you saw as a boy. Were you aware

3
00:01:41.000 --> 00:02:22.000
of the phenomenon that this was going to be when you started? No, absolutely not. Absolutely not. I was intimidated. We were all intimidated by the fact that the BBC had committed to a series of 100 programs. We had no idea how it was going to be received at all. And in a strange way, the project it began really almost as an internal project in the British Museum. What's interested me having come from the National Gallery, so a paintings collection, to a museum, the ambition of which was to show really the history of humanity through things, so not an art museum.

4
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:47.000
But to try to explore what it meant to put objects all over the world into one building. So it really began as a way of exploring what happens when you think of a museum as a place where different objects are exploring the same kind of human experience. And of all the things in the building, how did you choose the hundred?

